{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 conflates organizational independence with implementation heterogeneity, then smuggles the latter in as the load-bearing mechanism. Its own counterconditions concede that mandated frameworks and shared critical dependencies invalidate the claim — i.e., when small operators are independently run but homogeneous (precisely the common real-world case), the 'blast radius' decoupling vanishes. Under the problem's regime, 'one large service with strictly more total internal redundancy' can architecturally partition its redundant nodes across diverse stacks, deployment schedules, and infrastructures; its strictly greater redundancy can therefore achieve at least the same circuit-breaker effect SRC_001 attributes only to decentralization. No evidence is offered that a decentralized network can guarantee the implementation diversity its mechanism requires, so the resilience claim is unsupported against the stated comparator.","counterexample":[{"counter":"independently_operated_small_services_can_all_use_the_same_standardized_framework_or_DNS_provider_(SRC_001_counterconditions)","implication":"large_service_can_partition_strictly_more_redundancy_across_same_diversity_without_disadvantage","necessary_condition_for_resilience_claim":"guaranteed heterogeneous implementations","property":"strictly_less_internal_redundancy_than_large_service","result":"correlated_failure_not_decoupled","target":"decentralized set of small services"}],"target":"17f9dcec5035f828a3e497bbcb47761510216d26b9cc4d44cff9634fe7bbae67"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's 'Economic Convergence' attack is a misuse of the word 'strictly': it only argues that small operators tend toward market-driven homogeneity. The problem asks whether a large service with STRICTLY MORE total internal redundancy is more resilient than the decentralized network. A large service is subject to identical economic-convergence incentives — it likewise uses AWS/Kubernetes/industry-standard libraries. SRC_002's 'global kill-switch' vulnerability applies equally, without differential force, to the large monolith. Since the large service has strictly more total internal redundancy on the same common substrate, the mere existence of shared standard tooling does not make the small operators MORE prone; it neutralizes the scale difference and leaves the large service with an edge. SRC_002 never identifies a reason economic convergence bites small operators harder (e.g., their inability to afford independent verification stacks), so its evidence only distinguishes correlated-from-independent failures in general, not the targeted comparison.","counterexample":[{"conclusion":"SRC_002_fails_the_comparison_as_stated","result":"no_differential_shown;_strictly_more_redundancy_is_unaffected_by_choosing_the_same_standard_tooling","shared_vulnerability":"SRC_002_market-driven_homogeneity_(AWS/Kubernetes/standard_libraries)","target_a":"large_service_with_strictly_more_internal_redundancy_on_the_same_market-standard_stack","target_b":"small_independent_operators_on_the_same_market-standard_stack","test":"is_target_b_more_prone_to_correlated_failure_than_target_a"}],"target":"a30047b5d0c35a7d37f0e4ebb64e4533a06ecaa5d374aa2ed6269786e1b65be9"}]}